From f2516ac9a2d0bff479d2286f604796ecd88187b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Schachter <davidschachter@appdevforall.org>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:24:04 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] Report SQL errors from db.exec result sets

db.exec() prepares a statement and returns a result set; the statement
runs as next() or toArray() steps it. Both treated any sqlite3_step()
result other than SQLITE_ROW as the end of the rows, so an error while
the statement runs was dropped: next() returned false, as at the end of
the rows, and toArray() returned the rows read before the error. A
failed UNIQUE constraint on an INSERT, or an integer overflow, looked
like success, and JavaScript that writes through db.exec() could not
tell. An error found when the statement is prepared, such as an unknown
function, was already thrown by db.exec().

Throw an InternalError with the connection's error message when
sqlite3_step() returns neither SQLITE_ROW nor SQLITE_DONE, from next()
and from toArray(). The statement is finalized as before. The end of
the rows is still false. The README now documents db.exec(), including
that a statement runs only when next() or toArray() steps it.

Add an Exec errors test: a failed constraint and an overflow throw from
next(), an overflow on the second row throws from toArray(), JavaScript
can catch the error, and the end of the rows and a successful INSERT
are unchanged. It fails without this change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vh2DAXGyHbMzjSsi2zbrkw
